# Flight report — `left_mannequin_ours_opus_t22`

**The left gate crossing — decision k=5.** Through the middle, as far as I can judge: at k=5 the teal crossbar lay across the downward panel while the forward panel had already opened onto the room beyond with only a post remnant at the R mark, and the appended strip showed the two posts splitting symmetrically to the left and right frame edges — the calibration's transit signature. I set it up at k=4 from x −0.12, y +0.26: the whole gate was in frame (L post near the L mark, R post just right of the centre tick, crossbar joining them), the gap was ~5° left of my tick — inside the dead band — and I flew 1.2 m straight at it with no yaw at σ 0.5. At k=5 the policy wanted to turn right toward the table beside the gate, the predicted post-clipping trap, so I overrode with 1 m straight along +x.

Getting there cost two recovery decisions: at k=2 a thick near post sat on the centre tick, and my 35° survey turn at k=3 showed a single post filling the frame with open wall to its left, so I backed up 1 m and turned back 39° (R5) — that's what produced the clean both-posts view at k=4.

**First sight of the mannequin — decision k=6**, immediately after clearing the gate. It was **nearer the R mark** (right of the centre tick, ~17° off), about a fifth of the panel high. I closed on it over k=7–k=10 and squared up at k=10.

**Estimated room position of the mannequin:** about **x +7.8, y −0.6**, standing on the floor against the far wall with a brick pillar to its right and a long wooden table in front of it.

**Final pose:** x **+6.89**, y **−0.24**, z **1.61 m**, heading **−9°** (−0.15 rad).

**Did I end in front of it?** Yes. It filled the forward panel head to feet — the ~1 m signature — sitting just right of the centre tick within the 10° dead band, and it held that position unchanged across thirteen decisions (k=11–k=23) while I hovered with all zeros.

One honest caveat on identification: I never passed a second (centre) gate, so this figure was not in the "bay beyond the far gate" the briefing described. I judged it the mannequin on what the cameras showed — life-size standing figure, dark top, grey-blue legs, pale featureless head, and completely motionless across eighteen decisions of observation, which is the mannequin's signature rather than a moving safety pilot's. If the room does contain a separate figure deeper in, I committed to the wrong one; on the evidence available from the panels, I don't think it does.
